# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env — it is gitignored, and `docs/05-stack.md`
# explains why that rule is verified with `git check-ignore -v` rather than trusted.
#
# On the district's server this file IS the secret store, deliberately (ADR-0007, ADR-0011):
# one machine in the DC office, operable by one person at 02:00. A secrets manager would add
# a network dependency, an account and a renewal nobody is watching, to protect a file that
# sits on the same disk as the database it unlocks. What the file needs instead:
#
#   chown dnc:dnc app/.env && chmod 600 app/.env
#
# `src/config.ts` checks all of this at boot and says what is missing in one log line, rather
# than letting a mistake surface at 02:00 inside the backup job. Anything that would only make
# the district *less protected* warns and keeps running — a process that will not start is a
# district that cannot report an emergency (INV-01).

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

NODE_ENV=development

# The record itself. The process refuses to start without this, and refuses to start in
# production if it is still the line below.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/dnc_dev

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

PORT=3000

# error | warn | info | debug
LOG_LEVEL=info

# How often the escalation and notification passes run. Default 15000.
# ESCALATION_INTERVAL_MS=15000

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (ADR-0011)
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where nightly dumps are written. Defaults to app/var/backups.
# BACKUP_DIR=/var/lib/dnc/backups

# Needed only when `pg_dump` on PATH is the wrong version. On Debian /usr/bin/pg_dump is
# `pg_wrapper`, which picked 16 against a 17 server — see the CI workflow.
# PG_BIN=/usr/lib/postgresql/17/bin

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off-site backup — SECRETS. Waiting on the district (R-06).
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All three are needed together. With none of them set, nightly dumps still run and stay on
# this machine; `/health` and Administration → Backups both say plainly that no copy has ever
# left the district. With a bucket but no passphrase, the upload refuses every night rather
# than sending the district's record out unencrypted — that is deliberate, and the boot check
# warns about it so nobody discovers it after a fire.
#
# The passphrase must be at least 16 characters and is what a restore will need. If it is
# lost, every off-site copy is unreadable. Keep it somewhere that is not this machine.

# --- S3-compatible storage: Cloudflare R2, Backblaze, MinIO, or KPITB's own ---
#
# The district chose R2 on 2026-08-07 (ADR-0019). Set these four and the nightly backup goes
# there instead of Google Cloud. **Set S3_BUCKET and the S3 path is used**, even if the GCS
# values below are also filled in — configuring S3 is a choice, and silently preferring the
# older one would send backups somewhere nobody expected.
#
# For R2, from the Cloudflare dashboard → R2 → Manage API tokens:
#   S3_ENDPOINT       https://<your-account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   S3_REGION         auto        (R2 ignores it, but it is part of the signature)
#
# NOTE ON THE CLOCK: S3 refuses any request signed more than 15 minutes out. On a fresh VM
# run `timedatectl set-ntp true` — the error it gives otherwise says nothing about time.

# S3_ENDPOINT=
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_REGION=auto

# --- Google Cloud Storage: the original target, still supported ---
# GCS_BUCKET=
# GCS_TOKEN=

# BACKUP_PASSPHRASE=

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The district's own domain (R-21, ADR-0017)
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Do not fill these in by hand.** `npm run setup:domain -- --domain … --email …` checks the
# DNS record actually points here *before* changing anything, then hands over to the proxy
# installer which writes all four together. They are listed so somebody reading this file can
# see what is being set and why.
#
# The three below ship together or the third one is an outage: behind a proxy every request
# arrives from 127.0.0.1, so without TRUSTED_PROXIES the whole district shares one throttle
# key and one officer mistyping a password slows sign-in for everybody (M6-37).

# The address officers' phones can actually reach. Acknowledge links in WhatsApp messages are
# built from this — an office IP here is a link that fails on every handset it reaches.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://dnc.yourdomain.pk

# Loopback once a proxy is in front. A security boundary, not tidiness: reachable directly,
# X-Forwarded-For becomes a header the caller writes.
# HOST=127.0.0.1
# PORT=3001

# Empty means the X-Forwarded-For header is ignored entirely, which is the safe default and
# what every installation without a proxy keeps.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WhatsApp — SECRETS. Waiting on the district (R-05, R-19, R-20).
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **Do not fill these in by hand either.** `npm run setup:whatsapp` writes them, keeps a backup
# of this file, restarts the application so the values are actually read, and then runs
# `npm run doctor` to prove it works rather than announcing that it does.
#
# All four are needed together. With none of them set the system is complete and honest:
# obligations are recorded and immediately marked as needing a person, "Reach them" is there,
# the operator can record what they were told by telephone, and the dashboard
# carries a row saying WhatsApp is not configured — so nobody can believe messages are going
# out while they are not.
#
# Where each value comes from: `npm run setup:whatsapp -- --help`.

# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=

# Optional. Lets `npm run doctor` compare the approved template against what the software
# actually sends — a mismatch fails every send, at 02:00, as a provider error nobody can read.
# WHATSAPP_BUSINESS_ACCOUNT_ID=

# Only if the district approved the template under a different name or language. The wording
# to submit is in docs/whatsapp-template.md and must match what is sent.
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE=district_message_v3
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANG=en
#
# The template that carries a PICTURE (M10-28, M10-31)
#
# Meta will only put an image on a template message if that template was APPROVED with an IMAGE
# header. `district_message_v3` has none — two body parameters and one URL button — so a file
# reaches officers as a single-use link instead.
#
# Naming a template here is what turns pictures on, and it is chosen PER MESSAGE:
#
#   a JPG          -> this template, with the picture riding the message
#   a PDF          -> the ordinary template above, and a link in the words
#   no file at all -> the ordinary template above
#
# THIS REPLACED A BOOLEAN (`WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_HAS_MEDIA_HEADER`), AND THE REASON IS THE WHOLE
# POINT. That boolean said "our template has a media header" — one answer for every message. Set
# against the body-only template it would have attached a header to EVERY send, and Meta rejects
# a message whose components do not match what it approved: the emergencies would have failed
# alongside the meeting notice. Naming the image template instead makes that unreachable, because
# a header is only ever built for THIS template and this template is only ever chosen for a
# message that actually carries a picture.
#
# Leaving it blank is the ordinary state and breaks nothing. There is deliberately NO document
# template — the owner chose images only, so a PDF travels as a link permanently.
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_IMAGE=district_message_img_v2
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_IMAGE_LANG=en
#
# The templates an officer can answer with a TAP (approved 2026-08-19)
#
# Every template before these carried a URL button, so answering meant leaving WhatsApp: the
# handset opens an in-app browser and has to reach the district's server, on a district signal,
# at 02:00. A quick reply costs the officer no round trip — the tap arrives on this system's own
# webhook, the same path a typed reply already travels.
#
# Two, because the district asks two different questions:
#
#   WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_EMERGENCY -> "Acknowledge" plus an "Open details" link. Used for
#     emergencies, alerts, advisories and orders — the four kinds that carry an SLA clock.
#
#   WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE    -> "Attending" / "Not attending" / "Sending someone", and NO
#     link at all. Meetings only. Until this is set, an officer who cannot attend has no way to
#     say so except by typing.
#
# ⚠️ THE ACKNOWLEDGE LINK IS THE SECOND BUTTON ON district_emergency_v2. Meta matches a button
# parameter by position, so the index lives in src/ops/whatsappTemplate.ts beside the name it
# belongs to, and `npm run doctor` compares it against what Meta actually approved. A parameter
# sent to the wrong button is a message Meta refuses — every message on that template, not one.
#
# Each is read on its own: approval is per template, so the half that is approved starts being
# used the day it is. Leaving either blank is the ordinary state and breaks nothing — those
# messages simply keep going out on the ordinary template with one link.
#
# A message carrying a PHOTOGRAPH goes on WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_IMAGE instead of whichever of these
# it would otherwise have used — UNLESS a picture-carrying version of that specific template is
# also approved and named below (WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE_IMAGE / WHATSAPP_RESPONSE_IMAGE_
# CATEGORIES), in which case the picture rides IN that message and the buttons still come.
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_EMERGENCY=district_emergency_v2
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_EMERGENCY_LANG=en
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE=district_notice_v2
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE_LANG=en
#
# The per-category templates the district answers emergencies on (ADR-0034). One line, a comma
# list of category slugs — fire, medical, road_accident, rescue, security, flood, alert, advisory,
# order, schedule, information, other. A category not listed here goes on WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_
# EMERGENCY instead, exactly as before. Only categories whose dnc_response_<category> template is
# APPROVED UTILITY should be listed; `npm run doctor` grades each one.
# WHATSAPP_RESPONSE_CATEGORIES=fire,medical,road_accident,rescue
#
# THE PICTURE-CARRYING VERSIONS — 2026-09-04, the owner's own line: meetings, advisories, orders,
# schedules and information notices keep the photograph IN the message, with their own buttons,
# rather than trading it for a file link the way every other kind still does. Every other category
# (fire, medical, road_accident, rescue, security, flood, alert, other) is UNAFFECTED by these two
# lines and keeps sending its photograph as a link, on purpose — the owner asked for these five
# specifically, not all twelve.
#
# Each needs its own template approved at Meta first (npm run submit:template --name
# district_notice_img_v1, dnc_response_advisory_img_v1, dnc_response_order_img_v1,
# dnc_response_schedule_img_v1, dnc_response_information_img_v1) and is read independently of
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE / WHATSAPP_RESPONSE_CATEGORIES above — a district may have the
# ordinary version of a template approved and not the picture-carrying one, or the other way
# round, since Meta reviews each submission on its own schedule. Leaving both blank is the
# ordinary state and breaks nothing: every photograph then travels exactly as it does today.
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE_IMAGE=district_notice_img_v1
# WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_NOTICE_IMAGE_LANG=en
# WHATSAPP_RESPONSE_IMAGE_CATEGORIES=advisory,order,schedule,information

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Messages nobody asked for (Phase 5, 2026-08-21)
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ABSENT OR BLANK IS OFF, AND OFF MEANS NOT ONE MESSAGE. Leave this alone and the software
# behaves exactly as it did before Phase 5 shipped: it answers officers and it sends what the
# control room dispatches, and it never speaks first.
#
# Everything WhatsApp has ever sent from this system was ASKED FOR — an alert somebody
# dispatched, or an answer to something an officer typed into their own thread. This switch is
# the first code that messages a handset BECAUSE TIME PASSED. That is a different kind of thing
# to put on a district's number, so it is a decision for whoever owns the number.
#
# Three things, and each may be turned on by itself:
#
#   nudge    Before the escalation ladder climbs over an officer's head. They answered nothing,
#            so at the deadline the control room goes to their superior — and today the first
#            they hear of it is FROM that superior. Sent at two thirds of their allowance, with
#            the Acknowledged / On scene / Resolved buttons on it.
#   closed   "This is closed, thank you." To every handset told about the emergency EXCEPT the
#            officer who closed it, who has already been thanked in their own thread.
#   summary  One line a day for the DC and AC offices, at 08:00 in Bajaur, reporting the district
#            day that has just ended — the same sentence the morning report leads with.
#
# ALL THREE SEND ONLY INSIDE META'S 24-HOUR SERVICE WINDOW, so a handset that has never sent
# this number anything gets nothing, ever. That is the correct answer and not a limitation: the
# window is what makes these free, template-free and approval-free.
#
# COST AGAINST TIER_250: a nudge and a closing word cost NOTHING. Meta caps unique recipients in
# a rolling day, and both go only to handsets this district already messaged about that same
# emergency, inside that same day. The summary may cost one handset.
#
# `on`, `true`, `1`, `yes` or `all` turn all three on. `off`, `false`, `0`, `no` or `none` turn
# them off. A comma list turns on exactly what it names. A WORD THAT NAMES NOTHING TURNS NOTHING
# ON and says so in the boot log — `WHATSAPP_PROACTIVE=nudges` is a plausible thing to type and
# means nothing, and a false silence is a feature that did not arrive where a false send is a
# message on a real officer's handset at 02:00.

# WHATSAPP_PROACTIVE=closed
# WHATSAPP_PROACTIVE=nudge,closed,summary
# WHATSAPP_PROACTIVE=on

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Weather (R-16)
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Defaults to approximate coordinates for Khar (Bajaur HQ) — a default, not a finding. The district may
# want the reading taken at the DC office, or at a tehsil that floods first.

# WEATHER_LAT=34.7167
# WEATHER_LON=71.5167

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tests only
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The integration suite refuses to run against a fake and says so loudly if this is missing.
# Under CI a missing value is a hard failure, never a skip — otherwise one broken secret drops
# every integration test and the build goes green having run a fraction of them.

# TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5433/dnc_test
